</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document">Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:
<br class="ltx_break">Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an Exhaustive
<br class="ltx_break">Negative Result on Surgical Layer Selection</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ivan Pasichnyk
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>We Label Data Inc.
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ivan@welabeldata.com</span></a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><a href="https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8154-3345" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8154-3345</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The critical damping condition of the damped harmonic oscillator model of SGD with momentum (Qian, 1999) yields a momentum schedule with no tuned hyperparameters: <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(t)=1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(t)=1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> (a fixed, never-tuned stability clamp aside). We validate this schedule across five random seeds on ResNet-18/CIFAR-10 (200-epoch cosine learning-rate schedule): it reaches 90% test accuracy <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.34\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.34</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.34\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than constant <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> (range <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.71</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.71</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.86\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.86</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.86\times</annotation></semantics></math>, 5/5 seeds, one-sided paired <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=4\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=4\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>), at the cost of a real final-accuracy deficit of <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.46</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.46</annotation></semantics></math> pp (<math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5/5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5/5</annotation></semantics></math> seeds, <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.009</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.009</annotation></semantics></math>). A short-schedule control shows the speedup is not a schedule-length artifact: baselines with the cosine schedule compressed to 60/100 epochs either pay <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9</annotation></semantics></math> pp of final accuracy or remain slower to 90% than the physics schedule at equal accuracy—physics Pareto-dominates the accuracy-matched compressed baseline. A hybrid recipe—critical-damping momentum until 90% accuracy, then constant <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>—removes the deficit while keeping the speedup: <math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95.45\pm 0.05\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95.45</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95.45\pm 0.05\%</annotation></semantics></math> final accuracy (indistinguishable from baseline) at <math id="abstract1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.4\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster progress to 90% (<math id="abstract1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>). The speedup generalizes across architectures—VGG-16 without skip connections reaches 90% test accuracy <math id="abstract1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.72\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.72</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.72\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster (<math id="abstract1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math>)—while on CIFAR-100 the effect is smaller: early-training gains persist (<math id="abstract1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="abstract1.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> to mid-training thresholds) but the pure schedule pays a larger final-accuracy cost (<math id="abstract1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1.7</annotation></semantics></math> pp), compressing the accuracy-matched gain to <math id="abstract1.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.14\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.14</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.14\times</annotation></semantics></math> and delimiting the schedule’s scope.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We also report an exhaustive negative result on surgical layer selection. Version 2 of this paper claimed that gradient attribution computed on misclassified images selects <em id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> layers to retrain. Running the identical surgical-correction protocol on <span id="abstract1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">all 35 combinations</span> of 3-of-7 layer groups shows the diagnostically selected triple ranks 11th of 35 (exact <math id="abstract1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.31</annotation></semantics></math>)—no better than random selection. What survives is weaker but real: combinations containing the top-ranked layer outperform those without it (<math id="abstract1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+6.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+6.2</annotation></semantics></math> vs <math id="abstract1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1.4</annotation></semantics></math> mean net error reduction), and the <em id="abstract1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bottom</em> of the gradient-norm ranking reliably predicts the most harmful interventions (down to <math id="abstract1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-20</annotation></semantics></math> net errors). Gradient attribution on errors is a <em id="abstract1.2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harm-avoidance</em> signal, not an optimizer of layer selection. We release the full 35-combination landscape as a baseline we believe should accompany any layer-selection claim.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> momentum scheduling, critical damping, tuning-free momentum schedule, surgical fine-tuning, negative results, layer attribution</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Momentum and learning rate are usually tuned as independent hyperparameters. The damped harmonic oscillator model of SGD with momentum <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> says they should not be: for any learning-rate schedule <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, the critical damping condition fixes the momentum trajectory completely, <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(t)=1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(t)=1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, with no tuned hyperparameters (the implementation applies a fixed, never-tuned stability clamp; Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiment 1: Multi-Seed Validation of the Schedule ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). This paper asks two questions about that formula, and answers a third one we did not plan to ask.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Does it work?</span> Yes, with a quantified trade-off (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiment 1: Multi-Seed Validation of the Schedule ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Across five random seeds on ResNet-18/CIFAR-10 under a fixed 200-epoch cosine schedule, critical-damping momentum reaches 90% test accuracy <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.34\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.34</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.34\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than the standard constant <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> (range <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.71</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.71</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.86\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.86</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.86\times</annotation></semantics></math>, 5/5 seeds, one-sided paired <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=4\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=4\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>). The price is real, not seed noise: a final-accuracy deficit of <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.46</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.46</annotation></semantics></math> pp (<math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5/5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5/5</annotation></semantics></math> seeds, <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.009</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.009</annotation></semantics></math>). A short-schedule control (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Short-Schedule Control ‣ 4 Experiment 1: Multi-Seed Validation of the Schedule ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) rules out the schedule-length explanation: physics is faster to 90% than any compressed baseline of equal final accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Can the trade-off be removed?</span> Yes (Section <a href="#S9" title="9 Experiment 4: Hybrid Momentum Scheduling ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). A hybrid recipe—critical damping until 90% accuracy, then constant <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>—keeps the early speedup and recovers full final accuracy: across five seeds, Hybrid-90 reaches <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95.45\pm 0.05\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95.45</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95.45\pm 0.05\%</annotation></semantics></math> (statistically indistinguishable from the baseline’s <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95.49\pm 0.08\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95.49</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>0.08</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95.49\pm 0.08\%</annotation></semantics></math>) while hitting 90% in <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45.2\pm 9.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45.2</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>9.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45.2\pm 9.5</annotation></semantics></math> epochs—<math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.4\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than baseline, matching pure critical damping. The oscillator intuition explains why: near-critical damping is optimal for fast descent in the early high-curvature landscape, while the underdamped regime’s oscillation aids late-stage exploration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Does gradient attribution on errors select which layers to repair?</span> No—and version 2 of this paper claimed it does (Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Experiment 3b: The Exhaustive Layer-Selection Baseline ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). We ran the identical surgical-correction protocol on <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all 35 combinations</em> of 3-of-7 ResNet-18 layer groups. The diagnostically selected triple ranks 11th of 35 (exact <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.31</annotation></semantics></math>). Two weaker signals survive: combinations containing the top-ranked layer outperform those without it (<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+6.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+6.2</annotation></semantics></math> vs <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1.4</annotation></semantics></math> mean net error reduction), and the bottom of the gradient-norm ranking reliably identifies the most <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harmful</em> interventions (<math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-13</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-20</annotation></semantics></math> net errors). Gradient attribution on errors is a harm-avoidance signal, not a selector of optimal repair targets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Contributions, in order of the strength of the evidence:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-seed empirical validation</span> of the tuning-free critical-damping schedule: <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.34\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.34</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.34\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster to 90% (<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>, one-sided <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=4\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=4\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>), with an honest quantification of its final-accuracy cost (<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.46</annotation></semantics></math> pp, <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.009</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.009</annotation></semantics></math>). The formula is Qian’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>; the controlled validation and the trade-off quantification are ours.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Hybrid-90 recipe</span>: a practical schedule that dominates both of its parents (fast early convergence of critical damping, full final accuracy of constant momentum).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An exhaustive negative result</span> on error-gradient layer selection, with the full 35-combination landscape released as a baseline. To our knowledge no prior surgical fine-tuning work reports its selection rule against the complete combinatorial null.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ODE models of momentum.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Qian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> showed that gradient descent with momentum, in the continuous-time limit near a local minimum, is equivalent to a set of coupled damped harmonic oscillators, and that optimal convergence corresponds to critical damping of each eigencomponent. Su et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> derived an ODE for Nesterov’s accelerated gradient method. Shi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> introduced high-resolution ODEs distinguishing Nesterov’s method from heavy-ball momentum. <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Our work builds on Qian’s model: we derive the critical damping condition as a practical schedule and, more importantly, use damping-regime analysis as the foundation for a diagnostic pipeline.</em></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learning rate–momentum coupling.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Smith <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> empirically discovered that coupling momentum inversely to learning rate improves convergence (the 1cycle policy). <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Difference from our work:</em> 1cycle approximates the critical damping curve with a piecewise-linear schedule but does not derive the relationship from physics and does not use the framework for diagnostics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Physics-informed optimization.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Karoni et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> independently derive a momentum–learning-rate coupling from Hamiltonian mechanics, obtaining per-parameter adaptive damping (iKFAD, Cubic Damping, CADAM). Their framework is more general: per-parameter friction, cubic damping terms, bridges the Adam–mSGD gap on Transformers. <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Difference:</em> their work is an optimizer; ours is a diagnostic. We compare directly in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Experiment 3: Cross-Optimizer Invariance and Diagnostic Comparison ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and find that layer-level correction (ours and iKFAD-guided) outperforms their per-parameter correction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive methods.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> adapt the effective learning rate per parameter using gradient statistics. Polyak <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> introduced the heavy-ball method; Nesterov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> proposed a lookahead variant; Sutskever et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> established <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> as a practical default. Wang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> show that momentum’s marginal value diminishes for small learning rates—consistent with our analysis where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Closest to our schedule are momentum <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tuners and schedules</em>: YellowFin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> tunes momentum and learning rate jointly from a local quadratic model with a closed-loop controller; Sun et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> derive an adaptive momentum from quadratic optimization; Demon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> decays momentum over training by a fixed rule. <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Difference:</em> all three add machinery (estimators, controllers, or decay hyperparameters), whereas Eq. <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.3 The Critical Damping Schedule ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is a closed-form function of the existing learning-rate schedule with nothing to estimate or tune. We also note the classical exact result for quadratics—the optimal discrete momentum <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{*}=(1-\sqrt{\alpha\lambda})^{2}=1-2\sqrt{\alpha\lambda}+\alpha\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{*}=(1-\sqrt{\alpha\lambda})^{2}=1-2\sqrt{\alpha\lambda}+\alpha\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>—of which our schedule is the first-order (small-<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) approximation at unit curvature; and concurrent work on critically damped momentum near interpolation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Knowledge editing and model repair.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Meng et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> developed ROME and MEMIT for locating and editing factual associations in GPT models by modifying MLP weights in specific layers. <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Difference:</em> they edit specific facts via causal tracing; we diagnose general error patterns via gradient norms on misclassified inputs. Zou et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> proposed Representation Engineering, which reads and controls high-level concepts via population-level representations. <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Difference:</em> they identify <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">directions</em> in activation space; we identify <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">layers</em> where errors concentrate. Lee et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> showed that surgical fine-tuning of a subset of layers can match or outperform full fine-tuning for distribution shifts. <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Difference:</em> they showed <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">that</em> surgical fine-tuning works; we test a principled selection rule for <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which layers to target</em> and show, via an exhaustive 35-combination baseline (Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Experiment 3b: The Exhaustive Layer-Selection Baseline ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), that it does not outperform random selection — the rule’s reliable signal is harm avoidance. Recent work on hierarchical alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> decomposes objectives across functionally specialized layer blocks in LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background: The Oscillator Model</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reviews known results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and derives the explicit schedule used throughout this paper. The oscillator model itself is not our contribution; we include the derivation to set notation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>SGD with Momentum as a Damped Oscillator</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SGD with momentum updates parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> via:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mu\,v_{t}-\alpha\,\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mu\,v_{t}-\alpha\,\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\theta_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\theta_{t}+v_{t+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\theta_{t}+v_{t+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the velocity buffer, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the learning rate, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is the momentum coefficient, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the loss gradient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Eliminating <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by substituting Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 SGD with Momentum as a Damped Oscillator ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> into Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1 SGD with Momentum as a Damped Oscillator ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and taking the continuous-time limit, we obtain the equation of a forced damped harmonic oscillator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{x}+\gamma\,\dot{x}+\omega^{2}\,x=F(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{x}+\gamma\,\dot{x}+\omega^{2}\,x=F(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with the correspondence shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 SGD with Momentum as a Damped Oscillator ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Correspondence between the damped harmonic oscillator and SGD with momentum <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Oscillator</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SGD with Momentum</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Position <math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Parameters <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Velocity <math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Velocity buffer <math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Damping coefficient <math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Natural frequency <math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\propto\sqrt{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\propto\sqrt{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">External force <math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(t)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">stochastic/anisotropic gradient residual (see text)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second-order recurrence obtained by eliminating <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from Eqs. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 SGD with Momentum as a Damped Oscillator ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1 SGD with Momentum as a Damped Oscillator ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\theta_{t+1}-(1+\mu)\,\theta_{t}+\mu\,\theta_{t-1}=-\alpha\,\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}-(1+\mu)\,\theta_{t}+\mu\,\theta_{t-1}=-\alpha\,\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Near a minimum <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> we linearize <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta)\approx H(\theta-\theta^{*})+r(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta)\approx H(\theta-\theta^{*})+r(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the local Hessian and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(t)</annotation></semantics></math> collects the stochastic and anisotropic residual. For an eigenmode of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with curvature <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, dividing by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and identifying <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=(1-\mu)/\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=(1-\mu)/\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{2}=\lambda\alpha/\Delta t^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{2}=\lambda\alpha/\Delta t^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> recovers Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 SGD with Momentum as a Damped Oscillator ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, with the residual <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(t)</annotation></semantics></math> playing the role of the external force <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Setting <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=1</annotation></semantics></math> and unit curvature <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1-\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1-\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\sqrt{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\sqrt{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>; Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Spectral Grounding ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> discusses the general <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> case. The continuous-time identification is a consistent limit when <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\mu=O(\Delta t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\mu=O(\Delta t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=O(\Delta t^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=O(\Delta t^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. in the heavy-momentum, small-step regime typical of the schedules studied here.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.28921v3/fig_momentum.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/261;" width="476" height="261"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Momentum trajectories for the three methods under the 200-epoch cosine learning-rate schedule. The black dashed line is the unclamped critical-damping curve <math id="S3.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}=1-2\sqrt{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}=1-2\sqrt{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. The physics method (green, applied momentum) is clamped at <math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> through epoch 84 and tracks the critical curve thereafter.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Three Damping Regimes</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The behavior of a damped oscillator depends on the discriminant <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\gamma^{2}-4\omega^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\gamma^{2}-4\omega^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Underdamped</span> (<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&lt;2\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&lt;2\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>): the system oscillates around equilibrium before converging. In optimization, this manifests as fluctuating loss and accuracy curves.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overdamped</span> (<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;2\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;2\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>): the system approaches equilibrium without oscillation but unnecessarily slowly.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Critically damped</span> (<math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=2\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=2\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=0</annotation></semantics></math>): the unique regime that reaches equilibrium in minimum time without oscillation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>The Critical Damping Schedule</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Setting <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=2\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=2\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and substituting <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1-\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1-\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\sqrt{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\sqrt{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\mu=2\sqrt{\alpha}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\boxed{\mu(t)=1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow></menclose></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\mu=2\sqrt{\alpha}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\boxed{\mu(t)=1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given <em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> learning rate schedule <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, Eq. <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.3 The Critical Damping Schedule ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> determines a momentum trajectory that targets critical damping throughout training. We call this <em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-scheduling</em>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Spectral Grounding</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.3 The Critical Damping Schedule ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> sets momentum for a single-mode oscillator with unit curvature. A neural network loss has a spectrum of local curvatures <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, so one might ask why a global schedule works at all. Recent work by Olsen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> derives the stochastic dynamics of the squared singular values of weight matrices under SGD, showing that their stationary distribution is heavy-tailed with mass concentrated around a dominant scale that evolves slowly relative to the training trajectory. This gives a principled answer: our global <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(t)=1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(t)=1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> critically damps the <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dominant</em> spectral mode, while sub-dominant modes are left mildly under- or overdamped. The empirical robustness of Eq. <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.3 The Critical Damping Schedule ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> across runs and architectures reflects this separation of scales rather than a coincidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The natural refinement is a <em id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">per-eigenmode</em> schedule <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}(t)=1-2\sqrt{\lambda_{k}(t)\cdot\alpha(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}(t)=1-2\sqrt{\lambda_{k}(t)\cdot\alpha(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which critically damps every mode simultaneously. Implementing this requires an online estimate of the Hessian spectrum — tractable via Lanczos or Hessian-vector products, but nontrivial at scale. We defer the per-eigenmode construction and its joint analysis with Olsen et al.’s SDE to future work (see also Karoni et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> for a related per-parameter perspective). For the remainder of this paper, the global schedule is sufficient: the results in Sections <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiment 1: Multi-Seed Validation of the Schedule ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> onward show that damping the dominant mode already captures the phenomena of interest, without tuning <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> per layer or per parameter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Qualitative Predictions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The critical damping condition makes three testable predictions:</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fastest early convergence.</span> Critically damped systems have the shortest settling time.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No oscillation.</span> Training curves should improve monotonically during the high-LR phase.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Competitive final accuracy.</span> The oscillator model captures macroscopic dynamics but not the fine structure of the loss landscape. Final accuracy should be comparable but not necessarily superior.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Retroactive Explanations of Empirical Heuristics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.3 The Critical Damping Schedule ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> retroactively explains several established practices:</p>
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> is a common default.</span> At <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}=1-2\sqrt{0.01}=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>0.01</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}=1-2\sqrt{0.01}=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>. The value 0.9 is close—slightly underdamped but workable.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why 1cycle couples momentum inversely to LR.</span> Higher <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> requires lower <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> for critical damping. The 1cycle policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> approximates this curve.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why learning rate warmup helps.</span> Starting with small <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math> (near-zero damping), allowing velocity to build smoothly.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment 1: Multi-Seed Validation of the Schedule</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test the oscillator model’s qualitative predictions across five random seeds (42, 123, 456, 789, 1337). Runs are not bit-deterministic (cuDNN autotuning); repeated runs of the same configuration and seed can differ by a few hundredths of a pp in accuracy and a few epochs in milestones, which accounts for small discrepancies between experiments that reuse a configuration. The headline result is convergence speed (<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.34\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.34</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.34\times</annotation></semantics></math> to 90%, all seeds); the schedule also carries a real final-accuracy cost that single-seed experiments cannot distinguish from noise—we quantify both.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Multi-Seed Results</h3>

<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Multi-seed results (<math id="S4.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>, mean <math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> std): ResNet-18/CIFAR-10, 200 epochs, cosine LR <math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1\to 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1\to 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Nesterov and heavy-ball results (3 seeds) in the text.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best Test Accuracy</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Epochs to 90%</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Baseline (<math id="S4.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">95.49 <math id="S4.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.08%</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">107.8 <math id="S4.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 10.1</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">1cycle (Smith)</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">95.40 <math id="S4.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.05%</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">101.4 <math id="S4.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 16.0</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Physics (<math id="S4.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1-2\sqrt{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1-2\sqrt{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">95.02 <math id="S4.T2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.19%</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">47.0 <math id="S4.T2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 6.4</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speedup.</span> Physics reaches 90% before baseline on 5/5 seeds; per-seed speedups are <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.08\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.08</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.08\times</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.86\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.86</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.86\times</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.58\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.58</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.58\times</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.46\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.46</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.46\times</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.71\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.71</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.71\times</annotation></semantics></math> (mean <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.34\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.34</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.34\times</annotation></semantics></math>, min <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.71\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.71</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.71\times</annotation></semantics></math>). Friedman test across the three methods on epochs-to-90%: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=7.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>7.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=7.60</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.022" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.022</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.022</annotation></semantics></math>; paired <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test baseline vs. physics: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=9.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9.24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=9.24</annotation></semantics></math>, one-sided <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=4\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=4\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> (two-sided <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>) (Wilcoxon <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.031" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.031</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.031</annotation></semantics></math>, the exact floor at <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The accuracy cost is real.</span> Physics finishes below baseline on 5/5 seeds (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=0.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=0.46</annotation></semantics></math> pp, paired <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.009</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.009</annotation></semantics></math>). An earlier version of this paper described this gap as “within seed-to-seed variance”; the multi-seed data refutes that wording. The deficit is a property of the schedule—near-critical damping suppresses the late-training oscillation that helps escape shallow minima—and it motivates the hybrid recipe of Section <a href="#S9" title="9 Experiment 4: Hybrid Momentum Scheduling ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, which removes it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Momentum alternatives do not explain the speedup.</span> On 3 seeds, Nesterov momentum (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>) matches baseline accuracy (95.45–95.62%) with no early speedup (94–106 epochs to 90%), and heavy-ball <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.95</annotation></semantics></math> is worse on both axes (94.85–94.98%, 139–145 epochs).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Short-Schedule Control</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The comparison above holds the 200-epoch cosine schedule fixed for all methods, inviting the objection that a <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shorter</em> baseline schedule would reach 90% quickly too. We test this directly: constant <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> with the cosine schedule compressed to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\{60,100\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>60,100</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\{60,100\}</annotation></semantics></math> epochs, three seeds each.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Short-schedule control (<math id="S4.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math> seeds each) versus physics under the 200-epoch schedule (<math id="S4.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>). Compressed baselines trade final accuracy for speed; physics Pareto-dominates the accuracy-matched baseline.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Configuration</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Epochs to 90%</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best Test Accuracy</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Baseline, <math id="S4.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}60</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">38.7 <math id="S4.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.6</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">94.55 <math id="S4.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.02%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S4.T3.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Physics, <math id="S4.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}200</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">47.0 <math id="S4.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 6.4</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">95.02 <math id="S4.T3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.19%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Baseline, <math id="S4.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}100</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">58.3 <math id="S4.T3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 3.2</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">94.98 <math id="S4.T3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.07%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Baseline, <math id="S4.T3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}200</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">107.8 <math id="S4.T3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 10.1</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">95.49 <math id="S4.T3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.08%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Compressing the schedule is not free: the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}60</annotation></semantics></math> baseline reaches 90% in 39 epochs but caps at 94.55%—<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.47</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.47</annotation></semantics></math> pp <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">below</em> physics and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.93" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.93</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.93</annotation></semantics></math> pp below the full baseline (computed from unrounded means; the rounded table values give <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.94</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.94</annotation></semantics></math>). At matched final accuracy the ordering is unambiguous: the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}100</annotation></semantics></math> baseline achieves the same <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>95.0% as physics but needs 58 epochs to reach 90% versus physics’ 47. <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Physics Pareto-dominates the accuracy-matched compressed baseline</span>, and Hybrid-90 (Section <a href="#S9" title="9 Experiment 4: Hybrid Momentum Scheduling ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) dominates the full 200-epoch baseline the same way: equal final accuracy, roughly half the epochs to 90%. The speedup is a property of the momentum schedule, not of schedule length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this section reports the original single-seed (seed 42) experiment in detail, which the multi-seed run replicates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architecture.</span> ResNet-18 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> (11.2M parameters), modified for CIFAR-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>: <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> initial convolution with stride 1 and padding 1 (replacing the standard <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\times 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times 7</annotation></semantics></math>), max-pooling replaced with identity, final FC layer mapping <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512\to 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512\to 10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training.</span> 200 epochs, batch size 128, weight decay <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, seed 42, cosine annealing from <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\max}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\max}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\min}=10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\min}=10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Data augmentation: random crop (32, padding 4) and random horizontal flip.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware.</span> NVIDIA Tesla P100 (16 GB), PyTorch 2.4.1, CUDA 12.1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Three conditions.</span> All conditions use the same cosine LR schedule; only the momentum schedule differs:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Experimental conditions. All share the same cosine learning rate schedule.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Condition</span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Momentum Schedule</span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Free Params</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Baseline</th>
<td id="S4.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> (constant)</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">1cycle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="S4.T4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>: linear <math id="S4.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95{\to}0.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.95</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0.85</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95{\to}0.85</annotation></semantics></math> over the first half, back to <math id="S4.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> over the second</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">2</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Physics (ours)</th>
<td id="S4.T4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(t)=\text{clamp}(1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)},\;0.5,\;0.99)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>clamp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0.99</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(t)=\text{clamp}(1-2\sqrt{\alpha(t)},\;0.5,\;0.99)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Clamping.</span> The physics momentum is clamped to <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.5,0.99]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.99</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.5,0.99]</annotation></semantics></math> for numerical stability. At <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\max}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\max}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, the raw formula gives <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\approx 0.37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.37</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\approx 0.37</annotation></semantics></math>, clamped to <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.50</annotation></semantics></math>; at <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\min}=10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\min}=10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.98</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.98</annotation></semantics></math>, within bounds. The clamp bounds are fixed a priori and were never tuned; we count them as part of the method definition rather than free parameters, and Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3 Setup ‣ 4 Experiment 1: Multi-Seed Validation of the Schedule ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reflects tuned parameters. Incidentally, the exact discrete critical momentum for a unit-curvature quadratic is <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\sqrt{\alpha})^{2}=1-2\sqrt{\alpha}+\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\sqrt{\alpha})^{2}=1-2\sqrt{\alpha}+\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, which at <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\max}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\max}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4675" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.4675</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4675</annotation></semantics></math> — so the clamp at <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.50</annotation></semantics></math> keeps the applied momentum closer to the exact discrete value than the first-order formula itself during the high-LR phase. This means the physics model is <em id="S4.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> perfectly critically damped during the high-LR phase (approximately epochs 1–84, where <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0.063" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.063</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0.063</annotation></semantics></math> under the 200-epoch cosine schedule): it is slightly underdamped due to clamping. However, the deviation is much smaller than the baseline’s (<math id="S4.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mu=+0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.13</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mu=+0.13</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.53" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.53</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.53</annotation></semantics></math> at epoch 1). See Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 SGD with Momentum as a Damped Oscillator ‣ 3 Background: The Oscillator Model ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Results: Final Accuracy</h3>

<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Final accuracy (seed 42; all single-seed illustrations in this section use the committed raw data of the multi-seed run). The multi-seed analysis (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Multi-Seed Results ‣ 4 Experiment 1: Multi-Seed Validation of the Schedule ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) shows the physics deficit is systematic (<math id="S4.T5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.46</annotation></semantics></math> pp, <math id="S4.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.009</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.009</annotation></semantics></math>), not seed noise.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best Test Accuracy</span></th>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Time (s)</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Baseline (<math id="S4.T5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T5.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">95.45%</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">5,105</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">1cycle (Smith)</th>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">95.34%</td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">5,095</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Physics (<math id="S4.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1-2\sqrt{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1-2\sqrt{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="S4.T5.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">95.04%</td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">5,121</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All three methods achieve comparable accuracy within 0.41 percentage points on this seed. Training time is identical—the physics schedule has no computational overhead.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.28921v3/fig_convergence.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/272;" width="476" height="272"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Test accuracy during training (seed 42; multi-seed statistics in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Multi-Seed Results ‣ 4 Experiment 1: Multi-Seed Validation of the Schedule ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The physics method (green) converges fastest to intermediate thresholds, reaching 90% at epoch 51 versus epoch 106 (baseline) and epoch 112 (1cycle). All methods converge to comparable final accuracy.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Results: Convergence Speed</h3>

<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Epochs to reach accuracy thresholds (seed 42). The physics method shows a <math id="S4.T6.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.1\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.1\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup at 90%; the multi-seed mean is <math id="S4.T6.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.34\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.34</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.34\times</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Multi-Seed Results ‣ 4 Experiment 1: Multi-Seed Validation of the Schedule ‣ Critical Damping as a Momentum Schedule:Multi-Seed Validation, a Hybrid Recipe, and an ExhaustiveNegative Result on Surgical Layer Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</figcaption>
